# Naiad line-ending policy.  Added 2026-08-15 (queue 005 M3).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, in one case:
#
# The nine tracked *.sha256 sidecars under research_outputs/_archive/ were
# written on Windows with CRLF line endings.  `shasum -a 256 -c <file>` parses
# the line as "<hash>  <filename>", so the trailing \r became part of the
# FILENAME and every check failed with:
#
#     shasum: s1_2026-07-27.zip: No such file or directory
#     s1_2026-07-27.zip: FAILED open or read
#
# Nine perfect archives reported as nine failures.  The bytes were never wrong;
# the sidecars were unreadable by the standard tool on any POSIX host.  Anyone
# auditing the restore without knowing this would have concluded corruption.
#
# A checksum sidecar is a machine-parsed contract, not prose, so it is pinned to
# LF everywhere regardless of platform.  This is the class rule, not a one-off
# repair of those nine files.
*.sha256 text eol=lf

# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY THIS NARROW.  A first draft of this file also pinned
# *.py, *.json and *.sh to LF.  That is defensible policy and it was REVERTED
# unapplied, because eleven source files in this repo are still CRLF in the
# working tree: the rule would have re-normalised them wholesale, producing a
# ~15,000-line diff in the same commit as a targeted platform sweep and burying
# every real change in it.  Line-ending policy for source is its own decision,
# with its own commit and its own before/after suite run.  It is not something
# to smuggle in alongside other work.
#
# The eleven CRLF files, for whoever takes that decision:
#   scripts/{brief_render,census2a_program,census2a_viz,census2b_parta,
#            census2b_program,census2b_wtb1,mc2_program,wf1_forensics}.py
#   tests/{test_brief2_confluence,test_brief2_report,test_brief2_storage}.py
